;; read, checked and linked with musl by ci (bin/hsi cnodes: a node program) and loaded into go/arbace/ci over the
;; machine's own memory and system calls (crt's, which Mem and Sys already are), so a pointer or a file descriptor means
;; the same on both sides. A static foreign call of a symbol the C defines and hsi's table lacks is a call of ci's function
;; (C's stdio and allocator are musl's whenever a C program is loaded: cFirst); a label of a C object or function is its
;; address in ci's space; a dynamic call of an address of ci's text calls that function. hsi's own C functions have
;; addresses past ci's (funBase) and are natives of ci's program, so the C can call a pointer to one.


